            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the French for to land, then the French for to fly over."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: la turbulence",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la turbulence — \"the turbulence\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Pendant les turbulences, tout le monde reste assis. During the turbulence everyone stays seated."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Often plural: des turbulences."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "turbulence is a learned borrowing from Late Latin turbulentia, from turba 'disorder, crowd' (Lewis & Short). EtymDB does not list it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
